{"packet_id": "658b146c4f72b9df", "dimensions": {"problem_framing": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A1"], "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including no expansion, changing route to market and redesign, plus constraints on B2B relationships and reach to individual buyers. It does not correct a premise or surface a non-obvious constraint. Example use cases such as 'construction' and 'medical' blur consumer and B2B segments."}, "causal_mechanism": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "T1 (A9) names who changes behaviour, why, and how Hellberg captures value. The behavioural step ('fashion item and status symbol', 'purchase multiple pairs') is asserted without any grounding. So the chain is formally complete but not credibly traced."}, "distinct_theories": {"status": "scored", "score": 3, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "T1 (lifestyle accessory, specialty and direct-to-consumer channels, outdoor customers) and T2 (medical necessity, medical supply channels, hearing-sensitive customers) imply different customers, channels and products. Neither theory considers that there is no consumer demand at all."}, "assumptions_predictions": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "The predictions are observable (WTP premium of 20%/15%, purchase intent, repeat purchase). The assumptions are vague, e.g. 'associate hearing protection with outdoor lifestyle'. The thresholds are proposed assumptions, and it is not stated why failing them would sink each theory."}, "evidence_judgement": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15", "A16"], "reason": "Source classes are labelled and 'does not show' limits are given, but key sources are misread. A12 says the hlb-junior excerpt 'indicates a medical necessity for children', which the text does not say. A14 labels bogoch-2005 as